        "thinking": "Now I understand the structure. To run this locally with `wrangler dev`, I need to:\n\n1. Set up a `.dev.vars` file with necessary secrets/environment variables\n2. Handle the KV namespace ID (which currently says \"FILL_IN_AFTER_DEPLOY\")\n3. Handle Hyperdrive - in local dev, wrangler can simulate this with a direct postgres connection string via `HYPERDRIVE` in `.dev.vars` or via the `--test-scheduled` flag\n\nFor wrangler dev with local mode, we can:\n- Use `--local` to run completely locally (uses Miniflare)\n\n\n- Provide a real postgres connection string for HYPERDRIVE via `.dev.vars`\n- For the KV namespace ID, wrangler creates a local SQLite-backed KV store in local dev mode, so the placeholder ID doesn't matter\n\nI need to check what postgres database is available and what environment variables the server actually needs, since the `HYPERDRIVE` binding in wrangler.toml has `id = \"FILL_IN_AFTER_DEPLOY\"` and requires either a `localConnectionString` or proper configuration for wrangler dev to work.\n\nFor local development, I can configure wrangler.toml to use a local postgres connection by setting `localConnectionString` in the hyperdrive binding, which would let wrangler dev connect to a real database instead of trying to simulate Hyperdrive. Let me check what database setup the server uses locally.",
